# The question words, found from the answer they get.
lesson language-questions v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=7 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Asking questions"
  goal "Ask questions in [[language.name]] with where, when, who, what, how many, how, why and which."

  look {
    scene 38x14 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        comic ask who=["girl", "boy", "girl", "boy"] says=["[[question.where-do-you-live]]", "[[question.at-the-harbour]]", "[[phrase.how-many-brothers-and-sisters-do-you-hav]]", "[[question.two-a-brother-and-a-sister]]"] moods=["happy", "happy", "happy", "happy"] with=["", "", "", ""] thinks=[0, 0, 0, 0] holds=["", "", "", ""] labels=["1", "2", "3", "4"]
      }
    }
    say """
      A question word tells you what the answer will be: [[question.where]] asks where,
      [[question.when]] when, [[question.who]] who, [[question.what]] what, [[question.how-many]]
      how many, [[question.how]] how, [[question.why]] why, and [[question.which]] which.
      [[question.where-do-you-live]] gets a place: [[question.at-the-harbour]]
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.questions-name k=4
    }
    show language.questions-name k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=6
    show language.questions-meaning k=5 easy-k=4 hard-k=1
    show language.questions-write k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.questions-name k=5 easy-k=2 hard-k=7
    show language.questions-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.questions-write k=6 easy-k=3 hard-k=3
    show language.questions-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.questions-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.questions-write k=2
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-questions-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.questions-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[question.where|cap]] a place, [[question.when]] a time, [[question.who]] a person, [[question.what]] a thing, [[question.how-many]] a number, [[question.why]] a reason."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is where and when swapped, and why and because treated as one. Every
    naming question gives the answer first, which is the reverse of a usual question: the child has
    to know what kind of answer each word asks for. From this grade the written words are marked
    with their accents: a word typed without its accent is not taken. The dictation is read from
    this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or played by the device. Here every question word
    written carries an accent, which is the point of writing them. If the child is stuck, sort
    answers into piles (places, times, people, numbers) and name the question word for each pile.
    Off the page, play twenty questions in the language. It meets England's key stage 2 languages
    programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "engage in conversations; ask and answer
    questions"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign languages: "Can communicate in asking and answering
    questions about themselves, someone they communicate with and their surroundings by using simple
    words and phrases and basic expressions on the spot"; and CEFR A2, "Can communicate in simple
    and routine tasks requiring a simple and direct exchange of information on familiar and routine
    matters". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[question.how-many]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[question.where]] and [[question.who]] and the words to write are [[question.when-2]] and [[question.what-2]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[question.why]] and [[question.which]] and the words to write are [[question.how-2]], [[question.what-2]] and [[question.who-2]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
